  <content>
  <section>
<note>
  <para>Access the <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b62c9150250bf52d6cc37569213ab516e0090dca">Desmos guide PDF</link> for tips on solving problems with the Desmos graphing calculator.</para>


<para>For questions 1 - 2, write an equation in vertex form of the quadratic function that has a vertex at (5, 8) and also goes through the point \((-3, 6)\).</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><title>Activity</title><exercise id="_58610f1c-5083-4788-b112-383c4e6b3229" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Find the value of \(a\) for vertex form.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>\(y=a(x-h)^2+k\)
        \(6=a(-3-5)^2+8\)
        \(6=a(-3-5)^2+8\)
        \(6=a(-8)^2+8\)
        \(6=a(-8)^2+8\)
        \(-2=64a\)
        \(-2=64a\)
        \(a=\frac{-2}{64}=\frac{-1}{32}\)
        \(a=\frac{-2}{64}=\frac{-1}{32}\)</para><para>Nice try! The correct answer is \(\frac{-1}{32}\).</para></solution></exercise></section>
<!--Q#2-->
<!--Q#2-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Write the equation in vertex form.</para>
    <para>Select the <emphasis>Solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>\(y=\frac{-1}{32}(x-5)^2+8\)</para>

</solution></exercise>

<para>For questions 3 and 4, write an equation in vertex form of the quadratic function that has a vertex at \((-4, 3)\) and also goes through the point \((2, 5)\).</para>
<!--Interaction End -->
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="_3fb8876e-eb1f-4f46-99ef-2345f1eefa1b" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Find the value of \(a\) for vertex form.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>\(y=a(x-h)^2+k\)
        \(5=a(2-(-4))^2+3\)
        \(5=a(2-(-4))^2+3\)
        \(5=a(6)^2+3\)
        \(5=a(6)^2+3\)
        \(2=36a\)
        \(2=36a\)
        \(a=\frac{2}{36}=\frac{1}{18}\)
        \(a=\frac{2}{36}=\frac{1}{18}\)</para><para>Nice try! The correct answer is \(\frac{1}{18}\).</para></solution></exercise></section>
<!--Q#4-->
<!--Q#4-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Write the equation in vertex form.</para>
    <para>Select the <emphasis>Solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>\(y=\frac{1}{18}(x+4)^2+3\)</para>

</solution></exercise>

<!--Interaction End -->

<para>For questions 5 - 6, write an equation in vertex form of the quadratic function that has a vertex at \((6, -2)\) and also goes through the point \((3, 1)\).</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="_672d6cf9-11c0-41b0-8896-377ea42f89d5" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Find the value of \(a\) for vertex form.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>\(y=a(x-h)^2+k\)
        \(1=a(3-6)^2+(-2)\)
        \(1=a(3-6)^2+(-2)\)
        \(1=a(-3)^2-2\)
        \(1=a(-3)^2-2\)
        \(3=9a\)
        \(3=9a\)
        \(a=\frac{3}{9}=\frac{1}{3}\)
        \(a=\frac{3}{9}=\frac{1}{3}\)</para><para>Nice try! The correct answer is \(\frac{1}{3}\).</para></solution></exercise></section>
<!--Q#6-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Write the equation in vertex form.</para>
    <para>Select the <emphasis>Solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>\(y=\frac{1}{3}(x-6)^2-2\)</para>

</solution></exercise>

<!--Interaction End -->


<title>Video: Writing an Quadratic Equation from a Vertex and a Point</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more about writing a quadratic equation from a vertex and a point.</para>
<media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text"><iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/1IUEVwIjywc?si=lbcWBG5YBlTAOW1k;&amp;rel=0"/></media></section>

<note class="self-check">
  <para>Which of the following is the quadratic equation of the quadratic with a vertex of \((4, -2)\) that also goes through the point \((6, 5)\)?</para>
 </note>

 <section><title>Creating Vertex Form from a Vertex and a Point</title>
  <para>Write the quadratic in vertex form that has a vertex at \((3, 4)\) and goes through the point \((1, 2)\).</para>
  <para>First start with the vertex form of a quadratic function.</para>
  <para>\(y=a(x-h)^2+k\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Substitute the vertex in at \((h, k)\).
    \(y=a(x-3)^2+4\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Substitute the other point at \((x, y)\).
    \(2=a(1-3)^2+4\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Solve for \(a\).
    \(2=a(1-3)^2+4\)
    \(2=a(-2)^2+4\)
    \(2=a(4)+4\)
    \(2-4=a(4)+4-4\)
    \(-2=a(4)\)
    \(-\frac24=\frac{a(4)}{4}\)
    \(a=-\frac12\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Substitute the value of \(a\) value and the vertex \((h, k)\) into the vertex form.
    \(y=-\frac12(x-3)^2+4\)</para>
  
  <note class="try look-at-me"/>
  <title>Try It: Creating Vertex Form from a Vertex and a Point</title>
  
  <!--Q#-->
  <exercise>
    <problem>
      
      <para>Write the quadratic in vertex form that has a vertex at \((2, 5)\) and goes through the point \((3, 7)\).</para>
    
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>Solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
    
    </problem><solution>
    <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(y=2(x-2)^2+5\)</para>
    
    <para>First, start with the vertex form of a quadratic function:
      \(y=a(x-h)^2+k\)</para>
    
    <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Substitute the vertex at \((h, k)\):
      \(y=a(x-2)^2+5\)</para>
    
    <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Substitute the other point at \((x, y)\):
      \(7=a(3-2)^2+5\)</para>
    
    <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Solve for \(a\):
      \(7=a(1)^2+5\)
      \(7-5=a(1)^2+5-5\)
      \(a=2\)</para>
    
    <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Substitute the value of \(a\) and the vertex \((h, k)\) into the vertex form:
      \(y=2(x-2)^2+5\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
  <!-- Interaction End -->
  </section>
